  "text": "OLUTION\nLet us begin by denoting the coordinates of the vertices of the tetrahedron as $A(x_1, y_1, z_1),$ $B(x_2, y_2, z_2),$ $C(x_3, y_3, z_3),$ $D(x_4, y_4, z_4)$.\n\nFirst, observe that the product of the two different square roots of any three altitudes must be positive, considering that the altitude represents a distance from the opposite face.\n\nAssume the lengths of the altitudes are: $2\\sqrt{5},$ $2\\sqrt{15},$ and $2\\sqrt{65}.$\n\nThen, the product of these three is:\n\n$$2\\sqrt{5} \\cdot 2\\sqrt{15} \\cdot 2\\sqrt{65} = 16\\sqrt{225} = 16 \\cdot 15 = 240.$$\n\nThis product must also equal the product of the lengths of the fourth altitude squared, calculated as $2\\cdot 15 \\cdot 65$:\n\n$$2 \\cdot 15 \\cdot 65 = 1950,$$\n\nwhich confirms that $240$ is indeed a fitting number for the fourth altitude.\n\nNow, let us calculate the length of the fourth altitude as the square root of the product of the lengths of the other three:\n\n$$\\sqrt{240} = \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 15} = 4\\sqrt{15}.$$\n\nHence, the maximum possible length of the fourth altitude is $\\boxed{12\\sqrt{15}}$ units.",
